course: dictionary
lesson: 1836
title: A snake that warns
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cobra is a large, very poisonous snake from Africa and Asia. You can see its name is a noun, so you can say one cobra or two cobras. When you read about an animal, the words around its name give you clues about what it is like.
explain: «A cobra lifts the front of its body and spreads long ribs in its neck to stretch the skin into a hood.» The hood is a wide, flat shape made by the skin of the neck. Read on and you find out why: it is a warning that says «I am big, leave me alone». Spreading the hood makes the snake look bigger, so you can work out what it means from the clues.
explain: «The king cobra is the longest venomous snake, up to about 5 metres.» Longest and up to about 5 metres both tell you the king cobra is big. Venomous is a harder word, but the sentence says the king cobra is dangerous and poisonous, so you can guess it means full of venom. Always check: does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: A cobra is a large, very poisonous snake from Africa and Asia.
fact: When a cobra is in danger or angry, it spreads the skin of its neck into a wide hood.
fact: «The king cobra is the longest venomous snake, up to about 5 metres.»
fact: In stories, a cobra rising with its hood spread is a sign of danger.
fact: Never go near a wild snake, and always tell an adult if you see one.
exercise: What is a cobra? | A small, harmless lizard from Australia | A bird that lives high in the trees | A fish that swims in rivers | *A large, very poisonous snake from Africa and Asia
exercise: «A cobra lifts the front of its body and spreads long ribs in its neck to stretch the skin into a hood.» What does the hood warn? | *I am big, leave me alone | Come and play with me | It is time for dinner | I am lost and need help
exercise: Which word in «The king cobra is the longest venomous snake» tells you it is dangerous? | longest | snake | king | *venomous
test: What does a cobra do when it is in danger? | Hides its head under a rock | Sings a loud song | Runs fast into the long grass | *Spreads the skin of its neck into a wide hood
test: In stories, what does a cobra rising and spreading its hood mean? | *A sign of danger | A sign of a birthday party | A sign that it is sleepy | A sign that it wants a friend
exam: What is the safest thing to do if you see a wild cobra? | *Keep well away and tell an adult | Give it some milk to drink | Throw a stone at it to make it go away | Pick it up to look closer
